#From Gene Mutation to Disease:Investigating How DNA Sequence Changes Affect Protein Products and Human Phenotypes

Name: Rica Jane M. Torres
Disease: Tay-Sachs disease
Gene: HEXA
Documented Variant: NM_000520.6(HEXA):c.1274_1277dup

#Disease Background
Tay-Sachs disease is a rare inherited disease that mainly affects the nervous system. It happens when the body does not have enough working β-hexosaminidase
A (Hex A), an enzyme that helps break down GM2 ganglioside inside lysosomes. When the enzyme is missing or does not work properly, GM2 ganglioside builds up 
in nerve cells and causes damage. This can lead to delayed development, muscle weakness, seizures, and loss of vision and hearing (Lui et al., 2024).

The disease can have different forms, including infantile, juvenile, and adult-onset forms. Tay-Sachs disease is caused by mutations in the HEXA gene, which 
can result in reduced or absent β-hexosaminidase A activity (National Center for Biotechnology Information, 1998; Lui et al., 2024).

#Gene and Normal Protein Function
The official gene symbol is HEXA. The HEXA gene is located on chromosome 15, specifically at 15q23. It encodes the alpha subunit of the lysosomal enzyme 
β-hexosaminidase A. The alpha subunit is part of β-hexosaminidase A, which helps break down GM2 ganglioside and other molecules containing terminal N-acetyl 
hexosamines. This prevents GM2 from building up in cells, especially neurons. The β-hexosaminidase A enzyme is found in the lysosomes, where it helps with 
the breakdown and recycling of cellular materials. It participates in lysosomal lipid degradation, particularly the breakdown of GM2 ganglioside, together 
with the GM2 activator protein.

#Documented Mutation
The documented mutation used in this activity was: NM_000520.6(HEXA):c.1274_1277dup

This mutation involves the duplication of four nucleotides, TATC, in the HEXA coding sequence. The sequence changed from TATC to TATCTATC, adding four bases. 
Because four nucleotides were added, the mutation causes a frameshift. The predicted protein change is p.Tyr427fs. The frameshift changes the amino-acid 
sequence after the mutation and produces a premature stop codon. The variant is classified as pathogenic and is associated with Tay-Sachs disease.


#Hypothesis
If a mutation is made in the normal HEXA coding sequence, it is expected to affect the HEXA protein depending on the type of mutation. The documented HEXA 
c.1274_1277dup mutation is expected to cause a frameshift, change the amino-acid sequence, and create a premature stop codon. For comparison, the artificial 
three-nucleotide deletion is expected to remove one amino acid without changing the reading frame. These changes may affect the normal function of the HEXA 
protein.

#Methods
The normal HEXA coding sequence from NM_000520.6 was obtained from NCBI and uploaded to Galaxy as the Wild Type sequence. The WT sequence was translated using 
the Transeq tool to obtain its predicted protein sequence. The sequence and protein length, reading frame, start and stop codons, and amino-acid sequence were 
recorded. The documented HEXA c.1274_1277dup mutation was made using a copy of the WT sequence. The TATC nucleotides at c.1274–c.1277 were duplicated. The 
mutated sequence was then translated using Transeq and compared with the WT protein. For the artificial mutation, three nucleotides (GGC) were deleted from 
positions 71–73 of another copy of the WT HEXA sequence. The sequence was translated using Transeq. The WT, documented mutant, and artificial mutant sequences 
were compared based on their mutation type, amino-acid sequence, reading frame, protein length, and premature stop codon.

#Results
The WT HEXA coding sequence had a length of 1,590 bp and produced a predicted protein of 529 amino acids. The start codon was ATG, the stop codon was TGA, and 
the sequence was in Frame 1. For the documented mutation, the CDS increased from 1,590 bp to 1,594 bp because four nucleotides were duplicated. The first 
amino-acid difference appeared at amino acid 427. The mutation caused a frameshift and produced a premature stop codon. The predicted mutant protein was 431 
amino acids, making it shorter than the WT protein.

The artificial mutation involved deleting three nucleotides, GGC, from positions c.71–c.73. The CDS became 1,587 bp. Since three nucleotides were deleted, the 
reading frame remained unchanged, and the predicted protein was 528 amino acids.

#WT versus Mutant Protein Comparison
              	                 WT Documented         Mutant
Protein length	                     529 aa	          431 aa
Mutation	                            None	        c.1274_1277dup
Mutation type	                        None         	 Frameshift
First amino-acid difference           	-	          Amino acid 427
Reading frame	                       Frame 1	        Frameshift
Premature stop codon	                 No	                Yes

The WT and mutant sequences were the same up to approximately amino acid 426. Starting at amino acid 427, the sequence changed because of the frameshift. A 
premature stop codon was produced, resulting in a shorter predicted protein. The mutation did not directly delete or insert a single amino acid. Instead, the 
four-nucleotide duplication changed the reading frame and affected the amino acids that followed.

#Artificial Mutation Experiment
For the artificial mutation, three nucleotides, GGC, were deleted from positions c.71–c.73. Since three nucleotides were removed, the reading frame was expected
to remain unchanged because three nucleotides make up one codon. The predicted protein was expected to lose one amino acid while keeping the downstream 
amino-acid sequence in the same reading frame. Compared with the documented mutation, the artificial mutation was expected to have a smaller effect on the 
HEXA protein.

#Interpretation
HEXA c.1274_1277dup → TATC is duplicated → frameshift occurs → amino acids after position 427 are changed → premature stop codon → shorter HEXA protein → 
reduced β-hexosaminidase A function → GM2 ganglioside builds up in nerve cells → nerve cell damage → Tay-Sachs disease.

The c.1274_1277dup mutation duplicates four nucleotides, TATC, in the HEXA gene. This causes a frameshift in the coding sequence. Based on the Galaxy 
translation, the mutant protein first differs from the normal protein at about amino acid 427 and contains a premature stop codon. As a result, the predicted 
mutant protein is shorter than the normal 529-amino-acid HEXA protein. According to Lui et al. (2024), HEXA forms part of β-hexosaminidase A, an enzyme found
in lysosomes that helps break down GM2 ganglioside. When the enzyme is deficient or does not function properly, GM2 ganglioside cannot be properly broken down 
and accumulates in nerve cells. This buildup can damage neurons and contribute to the neurological problems associated with Tay-Sachs disease.

#Limitations
This activity was based on sequence analysis and protein translation using Galaxy. The results showed the DNA change, frameshift, amino-acid changes, 
premature stop codon, and predicted protein length. However, the analysis could not directly prove that the mutant protein is nonfunctional or that GM2 
ganglioside actually accumulates in nerve cells.

The activity also did not directly measure β-hexosaminidase A activity. The artificial mutation was only created for comparison and was not presented as a
known disease-causing mutation. Published experimental and clinical evidence is needed to confirm the actual effects of the mutation in cells.

#Conclusion
The activity showed how a change in the DNA sequence can affect the predicted protein produced by a gene. The documented HEXA c.1274_1277dup mutation duplicated
four nucleotides, TATC, which caused a frameshift starting at about amino acid 427. This changed the downstream amino-acid sequence and produced a premature
stop codon, resulting in a shorter predicted HEXA protein. In comparison, the artificial three-nucleotide deletion was expected to keep the reading frame
unchanged and remove only one amino acid. The results showed that the number and type of nucleotides changed can affect the resulting protein in different ways.

To sum it up, the activity helped connect the HEXA mutation to changes in the predicted protein and its possible effect on β-hexosaminidase A function, which
is related to the development of Tay-Sachs disease. The computational results also showed why sequence changes need to be connected to biological evidence when
explaining how a mutation contributes to disease.

